## Poll Everywhere Question 1 


## How can we model life expectancy with cell phones and income level?

::: columns
::: {.column width="10%"}
:::

::: {.column width="80%"}
::: definition
::: def-title
Simple linear regression population model
:::

::: def-cont
$$\begin{aligned}
\text{LE} & = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{CP} + \epsilon
\end{aligned}$$
:::
:::

::: fact
::: fact-title
Multiple linear regression population model (with added income level)
:::

::: fact-cont
$$\begin{aligned}
\text{LE}  = & \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{CP} + \beta_2 I(IL = \text{``Lower middle"}) + \\ & \beta_3 I(IL = \text{``Upper middle"}) + \beta_2 I(IL = \text{``High"}) + \epsilon
\end{aligned}$$
:::
:::
:::

::: {.column width="10%"}
:::
:::

## How do we fit a multiple linear regression model in R?

[New **population** model for example:]{style="color: #4FADF3;"}

$$\text{LE}  = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{CP} + \beta_2 I(IL = \text{``Lower middle"}) + \beta_3 I(IL = \text{``Upper middle"}) + \beta_2 I(IL = \text{``High"}) + \epsilon $$


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Code: Use lm() to fit model and then tidy() to display regression table"}
mr1 <- gapm %>% lm(formula = life_exp ~ cell_phones_100 + income_level_4)
tidy(mr1, conf.int=T) %>% gt() %>% tab_options(table.font.size = 35) %>% fmt_number(decimals = 3)
```

